# One Little Book — onelittlebook.com
#
# The shop's name, domain and support address live in `lib/brand.ts` and nowhere
# else. Nothing in this file needs to know them.
#
# THE REPOSITORY IS PUBLIC. Nothing in this file ever carries a value: it names
# the variables and says what each one is for, and the values live in Vercel's
# environment settings and in a local `.env.local`, which `.gitignore` excludes.
#
# With none of it set the app runs, renders the page and writes the print file,
# serving the fixture photographs out of `fixtures/assets/` — inert rather than
# broken, which is the arrangement Keepnook uses for EXPO_PUBLIC_CONTENT_ORIGIN.
#
# TWO THINGS ARE NO LONGER OPTIONAL IF YOU WANT TO TAKE MONEY. `lib/checkout.ts`
# § REQUIRED gates the whole money path: the Stripe key, the webhook secret and
# the four R2 variables the ORDER store needs — R2_ACCOUNT_ID, R2_BUCKET,
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID, R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY. Any one of them empty and the checkout
# refuses, says nothing has been charged, and names the rows to fill. That is
# the only honest state for a shop that cannot record or confirm an order. The
# ARTWORK rows are not in that list: a shop with no artwork still sells a
# complete, printable book.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare R2 — THREE BUCKET ROWS, AND THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THEM IS THE
# POINT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything this shop stores is in R2: the orders, the buyers' photographs,
# the looks' finished artwork and the per-order artwork made from a photograph.
# `lib/artwork/store.ts` § THE DECISION has the numbers that moved the artwork
# off Vercel Blob and `lib/artwork/r2.ts` § TWO BUCKETS AND NEVER ONE is the
# safety argument for the shape below.
#
#   R2_BUCKET            PRIVATE  orders/<ref>.json and photos/<sha256>.<ext> —
#                                 a customer's name, address, email and a
#                                 photograph of their own child.
#
#   R2_ORDERS_BUCKET     PRIVATE  one order's regenerated artwork, made by hand
#                                 from that photograph. No URL exists for any of
#                                 it; the only way to see one is a server read
#                                 behind /admin.
#
#   R2_LOOKS_BUCKET      PUBLIC   the looks' pages, the portrait tiles, the
#                                 golden ghost halves and the covers — the same
#                                 pictures for every buyer, shown to anybody who
#                                 opens the preview with no watermark and no
#                                 account. Public so a twenty-five page preview
#                                 is twenty-five CDN reads and not twenty-five
#                                 function invocations.
#
# THE TWO PRIVATE ROWS MAY NAME THE SAME BUCKET. Their prefixes do not collide
# — orders/, photos/, assets/, sets/ — and both are private, so there is nothing
# to get wrong. They are two rows because an order's artwork is tens of
# megabytes and an order record is a kilobyte, and the day that wants its own
# bucket it is a value and not a migration.
#
# THE PUBLIC ROW MAY NEVER NAME EITHER OF THEM, and this is the one
# misconfiguration that would look like it worked. A Cloudflare custom domain
# is attached to a BUCKET and not to a prefix: point one at a bucket holding
# orders/ and every customer's name and address is readable by anybody who can
# walk a key. `looksBucketIn` refuses that collision, names the row that
# collided, and falls back to the local directory — a shop with no artwork is a
# state this repository is built to be fine in; a CDN serving a customer's child
# is not. test/artwork-separation.test.ts asserts it.
#
# ONE API TOKEN COVERS ALL OF IT. The account id, the key and the secret below
# are shared, which is the captain's decision: there is one Cloudflare account
# and one token with read-write over both buckets. That is also exactly why the
# bucket-name collision is refused in code — a copy-paste in a dashboard is how
# the two would become one, and the credential would not notice.
#
# AN ACCOUNT ID IS A CREDENTIAL for this purpose and belongs here with the rest
# of them. All four of the orders rows are in `REQUIRED` in lib/checkout.ts, so
# with any of them empty the checkout refuses rather than taking money for a
# book it cannot record. The artwork rows are NOT in that list, deliberately: a
# shop with no artwork draws every page from the numbers in
# templates/first-book.ts and sells a complete, printable book.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# The private bucket the order records and the photographs are in.
R2_BUCKET=

# The private bucket one order's regenerated artwork is in. Unset, the per-order
# artwork store falls back to a directory on this machine and SAYS SO on /admin.
# Half-set — a bucket with no key, or a key with no bucket — is its own state
# and names the empty rows, because that is the one that looks configured from a
# dashboard and is not.
R2_ORDERS_BUCKET=

# The PUBLIC bucket the looks' artwork is in, e.g. onelittlebook-looks.
#
# `npm run artwork:upload` writes here and the deployed shop reads it on the
# next request, with nothing to redeploy. Unset, both read and write fall back
# to the local directory in RELEASE_ARTWORK_DIR below.
R2_LOOKS_BUCKET=

# The custom domain attached to R2_LOOKS_BUCKET, e.g. https://art.onelittlebook.com
#
# READS COME FROM HERE AND NEVER FROM A SIGNED URL. The looks' pages are
# deliberately public — the whole-book preview shows every page to everybody
# with no account — so serving them straight from the CDN is the point rather
# than a shortcut. That is also why this row is not optional: with it empty
# there is no public bucket at all and the local directory answers instead.
#
# DO NOT PUT THE r2.dev ADDRESS HERE EXCEPT FOR A LOCAL SMOKE TEST. Cloudflare
# rate-limits the default per-bucket address and does not intend it for
# production traffic. It is accepted rather than refused — a smoke test is a
# real thing to want — and `describe` says out loud on /admin that it is what is
# configured.
#
# IT IS NOT R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL BELOW, and the two are not interchangeable. This
# one fronts the looks bucket and serves `assets/<sha256>.png`,
# `sets/<set>/vNNNN.json` and `screen/<sha256>-wNNNN.webp`
# (`lib/artwork/object-store.ts` § The layout). That one is the flat
# `<sha256>.<ext>` keyspace `lib/assets/resolve.ts` reads for a photograph, is
# populated by nothing today, and must not be pointed at a bucket holding
# orders/ for the reason above.
R2_PUBLIC_BASE=

# Public read base for the flat asset keyspace — `<sha256>.<ext>` at the root of
# whatever bucket it fronts. Unset, assets are served from fixtures/assets via
# /api/assets, which is the state everything runs in today.
#
# A CUSTOMER'S OWN PHOTOGRAPH IS NOT SERVED FROM HERE, and that is a decision.
# A photograph of somebody's own baby goes under the private `photos/` prefix in
# R2_BUCKET and is read back with a signed request, so it is never a public URL.
# See `lib/orders/store.ts` § Photographs are private.
R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stripe — payments
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE MONEY PATH IS GATED AS A WHOLE, in `lib/checkout.ts`, and `REQUIRED`
# there is the list: both Stripe variables below and all four R2 variables
# above. With any one of them empty `/api/checkout` answers 503 and `/checkout`
# says plainly that nothing has been charged — never a crash and never a fake
# success.
#
# Why the whole list and not just the key: a shop with a Stripe key and nothing
# behind it is the WORSE failure. The card is charged on Stripe's own page and
# then, with no webhook secret, no event can ever be verified so nothing may
# mark the order paid; and with no bucket the order is written nowhere and the
# customer is owed a book nobody can print. Both are money taken for a book
# that does not get made.
#
# A blank row, a placeholder, or a pasted variable NAME all count as unset: see
# `isSet` in lib/env.ts and the cases in test/checkout.test.ts. That is
# deliberate, because those are what a dashboard actually contains after a
# hurried first deploy, and each one would otherwise read as configured and
# fail further in.
#
# Set them in Vercel's environment settings. Nobody shares a key with anybody,
# and no value belongs in this file.

# The secret key. `sk_test_…` is reported as its own state rather than as
# ready — a shop whose live button charges nobody is the other way to lie by
# accident — and the whole path works in test mode, which is how to try it.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# The signing secret for the webhook endpoint, `whsec_…`, copied out of
# Stripe's dashboard for the `/api/stripe/webhook` endpoint. THE WEBHOOK IS THE
# ONLY THING THAT MAY MARK AN ORDER PAID: a buyer landing back on the site is a
# URL they could have typed, so `app/api/stripe/webhook/route.ts` verifies this
# signature and nothing else in the codebase writes `payment: "paid"`.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Not read by anything. The hosted Checkout Session is created server-side and
# the buyer is redirected to Stripe's own page, so the browser never needs a
# Stripe key at all. Named here so nobody invents a home for it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The owner's page — /admin
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ONE SHARED SECRET, AND IT FAILS CLOSED. With this unset nobody can reach the
# order list, an order, a print file or a customer's photograph — not "it is a
# bit open", nobody. That is the opposite of the arrangement everywhere else in
# this file, where an unset variable means a feature politely does nothing;
# behind /admin is every customer's address and every customer's photograph of
# their own child, so the default is nobody. `lib/admin/guard.ts` says so and
# `test/admin-guard.test.ts` asserts it.
#
# It is not an account system and does not pretend to be one: no user, no
# expiry beyond the cookie's own, and no revocation except changing this value.
# Make it long and random.
ADMIN_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email — the order confirmation
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The order confirmation is the only thing from this business that every buyer
# keeps, and `lib/email/order.ts` is its text. UNSET IS A HONEST STATE, not a
# broken one: the order is still recorded and still paid, the webhook still
# answers 200, and the record carries `emailed: false` — which the owner's page
# shows as "CONFIRMATION NOT SENT" so it is a thing somebody can act on rather
# than a silent zero.
#
# Resend's API, one HTTP call, no SDK. Swapping the provider is lib/email/send.ts.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# The From address, e.g. orders@onelittlebook.com. The domain has to be
# verified with the provider or nothing sends.
ORDER_EMAIL_FROM=

# Optional. The address the 24-hour change window tells buyers to write to. With
# it unset the email says "reply to this email" instead, which always works —
# the promise is never left without a door.
ORDER_EMAIL_REPLY_TO=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where this deployment is, for the URLs Stripe sends buyers back to
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Optional. Stripe needs absolute URLs and the origin of the incoming request
# is the right answer on Vercel — a preview deployment then returns to itself
# rather than to production. Set this only when the request's own origin is
# wrong, which means a proxy in front of the app.
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vercel Blob — RETIRED, AND NOTHING HAS BEEN DELETED FROM IT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The artwork was in two Vercel Blob stores until the public one filled up:
# `olb-looks` held 1.03 GB across 331 files against a 1 GB limit, and the last
# upload landed a look's masters and not its screen copies. Fourteen looks at
# twenty-four pages is about 2.5 GB, so it was never going to fit at any point.
# `olb-orders` followed because it was empty — no data to migrate, and one
# provider is one credential set and one adapter. `lib/artwork/store.ts`
# § THE DECISION is the whole argument.
#
# NEITHER VARIABLE BELOW IS READ BY ANYTHING ANY MORE, and `@vercel/blob` is no
# longer a dependency. They are still named here for two reasons: they are
# probably still set on the Vercel project, and **the data is still sitting in
# `olb-looks`** — nothing has been deleted from either store, which is a thing
# to do deliberately with the captain once R2 is serving and not as a side
# effect of a migration.
#
# ONE HAZARD WENT AWAY WITH THEM, and it is worth recording because it shaped
# the code that replaced it: `@vercel/blob` falls back to BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN
# — the PUBLIC store's token — when given no other credential, so a private
# write on a machine with no OIDC token would have authenticated against
# `olb-looks` and put a photograph-derived page of somebody's child in the
# public store. The R2 adapter has no credential search of any kind: every
# request is signed with exactly what it was handed. The equivalent hazard on
# R2 is a public bucket that is also a private bucket, and that one is refused
# in code — see R2_LOOKS_BUCKET above.

# The read-write token for the old public store, olb-looks. Read by nothing.
BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=

# The store id of the old private store, olb-orders. Read by nothing.
OLB_ORDERS_STORE_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-order artwork — the photo path's hand-made character swap
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHERE THE REGENERATED ARTWORK FOR ONE ORDER IS KEPT, and it is not a secret:
# it is a directory. Unset it writes to `.artwork/` beside the repository,
# which `.gitignore` excludes.
#
# NOTHING HERE MAY EVER BE COMMITTED. It is a picture of one family's child,
# this repository is public, and git history is permanent. Point this anywhere
# outside the working tree if that is easier to be sure of.
#
# IN PRODUCTION THIS IS NOT THE ANSWER — R2_ORDERS_BUCKET above is. A Vercel
# function's filesystem is read-only outside /tmp, so the local store is right
# for the captain running this on their own machine, where a forty-megabyte
# artwork swap actually happens, and it is not production. It is also not a
# silent fallback: /admin prints which store it is reading, and the sentence it
# prints names this directory when it is this directory.
ORDER_ARTWORK_DIR=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The captain's released artwork — the looks, the portraits, the golden halves
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHERE THE DELIVERED ARTWORK IS KEPT, and it is not a secret either: it is a
# directory. Unset it writes to `.artwork/release/`, inside the same ignored
# directory the per-order artwork uses, so one .gitignore rule covers both.
#
# NONE OF IT IS EVER COMMITTED. One look of twenty-four finished pages is
# 155 MB and fourteen looks is about two and a half gigabytes; this repository
# is public and a clone is forever. `docs/artwork.md` is the inventory and
# `lib/artwork/release.ts` says why not even the manifest is committed.
#
# WITH NOTHING IMPORTED THE SHOP STILL WORKS, which is the point: every page is
# drawn from the numbers in templates/first-book.ts and the print file is a
# genuine 3600 px file. `npm run artwork -- status` says what this machine has.
#
# IN PRODUCTION THIS IS NOT THE ANSWER EITHER — R2_LOOKS_BUCKET above is. With
# that bucket configured, `npm run artwork` and `npm run artwork:upload` both
# write to it and the deployed shop reads it on the next request, with nothing
# to redeploy. With it unset, this directory is where the artwork goes and the
# real pictures are on one laptop.
RELEASE_ARTWORK_DIR=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Printify — uploading the print file against an order. A later task.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

PRINTIFY_API_TOKEN=
PRINTIFY_SHOP_ID=
